const oregano=R('Origanum vulgare subsp. hirtum',null,'sun','Acid, neutral or alkaline; no numerical optimum established','dry',
 ['Container option: Scotts Osmocote 25L Tomato Vegetable & Herb Premium Potting Mix (Bunnings)','Product match to the sourced herb-potting requirement, not an ABC/RBG brand endorsement. Use a pot with drainage holes; do not compact the mix or leave the pot standing in water. For garden beds, retain well-drained existing soil; this bag is not a mandatory amendment. The mix contains controlled-release fertiliser: check the actual pack before adding more feed.'],
 ['Only when extra feeding is needed: Yates 500mL Thrive All Purpose Liquid Plant Food (Bunnings)','RHS supports general-purpose liquid feed for container Mediterranean herbs at group level. This product is labelled for herbs; follow its current dilution and application directions rather than treating the RHS frequency as its label. Fresh fertilised potting mix may already supply nutrients, so do not automatically add liquid feed. Avoid feeding dry or moisture-stressed plants. No tested Greek-oregano dose or mandatory in-ground feeding schedule established.'],
 'Harvest sprigs regularly and trim spreading growth. For culinary leaves, shear before flowering; leave some flowers if supporting pollinators.',
 'Only water when the container mix has dried; inspect moisture before deciding. Inspect sooner in hot, windy weather, small pots or after planting; check again sooner if the mix is still moist at the reminder. Only water when the container mix has dried, then drain freely. A damp mix or recent effective rain can mean no water is needed. Adjust checks to actual drying and avoid persistent wetness.',
 'A sunny position with well-drained soil. Sun-grown Origanum leaves have a stronger flavour than those grown in shade. Practical application indoors: provide a sunny position.',
 'Low-growing, spreading aromatic perennial. Hairy, gland-dotted leaves and white flowers distinguish Greek oregano; ABC notes it can spread to about a metre.',
 'Seed or division. ABC documents seed propagation for Origanum species and potting up rooted oregano divisions; the division example does not identify the subspecies.',
 'Wet, poorly drained soil can cause root rot. Monitor for aphids, leaf miners, spider mites and leafhoppers; fungal diseases can occur. No pesticide or fungicide is prescribed: any treatment must match an APVMA-approved edible-herb use and label directions, including harvest restrictions.',
 'Altona/Melbourne interpretation: nearby Laverton RAAF averages show cooler winters and rain in every month. Check actual soil moisture and drainage through cool/wet periods; inspect pots more often in heat or drying wind. Greek oregano is a hardy perennial, not a frost-tender tropical herb. Use local forecasts for cold/frost exposure; climate averages are not a forecast or a subspecies damage threshold.',
 'If oregano crowds its pot or the mix is depleted, divide excess rooted growth and refresh the potting mix while retaining drainage. No fixed repotting interval established.',
 'Regular tip harvesting or trimming can keep growth compact. No mandatory pinching height or frequency established.',
 'ABC Gardening Australia: Thyme for Pizza; Origanum (Plant Finder, credited to Flora’s Gardening Cards / Global Book Publishing); Herb Your Enthusiasm. RBG Victoria HortFlora: Origanum vulgare (subsp. hirtum identification). BOM: Laverton RAAF climate averages (regional context). APVMA: Understanding pesticide labels (treatment constraints). Supplementary NC State Extension: exact subspecies moisture, pH categories, pruning and pests; RHS: Mediterranean-herb container watering/feeding. ANBG searched; no useful subspecies care profile located.');
oregano.reviewedAt='2026-10-06';
